== ws
set up the server and the Mac that works on it

Usage:
  ws [flags]
  ws [command]

Available Commands:
  client      the client's systems
  help        Help about any command
  server      the server's systems
  unit        plan and apply the units of ws's kinds, KIND/NAME, for mise
  update      update ws to the latest release
  version     print the version of this ws

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for ws

Use "ws [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client
the client's systems

Usage:
  ws client [flags]
  ws client [command]

Available Commands:
  macos       the commands for a Mac

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for client

Use "ws client [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client macos
the commands for a Mac

Usage:
  ws client macos [flags]
  ws client macos [command]

Available Commands:
  apply       set the Mac up from values.yml and a server's file
  remove      remove a server from the Mac
  resolver    send DOMAIN's names to the nameservers, or drop it
  servers     list the Mac's servers with their target, slot and tunnel
  state       report what the Mac's steps would change
  tunnel      the Mac's tunnel to ws
  values      print the Mac's values

Flags:
  -h, --help            help for macos
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one

Use "ws client macos [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client macos apply
It runs the steps it names, from values.yml and the server's file, in this order: ssh, cable, git, ghostty, zed, cld, zsh, tunnel, networks. With no --only, it runs every step, and ssh again, whose plan is empty unless cld changed ssh's file. It asks sudo where it needs root.

Usage:
  ws client macos apply [flags]

Flags:
      --check          print each change and make none
  -h, --help           help for apply
      --only strings   run the named steps alone, as a list or by repeating the flag

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos remove
It deletes servers/NAME beside values.yml and NAME's entry in known_hosts, and leaves the server as it is. It refuses the Mac's last server, as apply needs one, and takes no --server: the argument is the consent.

Usage:
  ws client macos remove NAME [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for remove

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos resolver
It has macOS ask the nameservers for DOMAIN, or drop the file of DOMAIN where it gets none.

Usage:
  ws client macos resolver DOMAIN [ADDRESS...] [flags]

Flags:
      --check   print each change and make none
  -h, --help    help for resolver

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos servers
It lists each server of servers/ beside values.yml: its target, its slot, and its tunnel, which is absent, stopped, starting, unreadable, or the tier and endpoint it holds. A server whose file ws refuses shows - and its errors follow the list, with exit code 1. It reads alone, as the user, and takes no --server.

Usage:
  ws client macos servers [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for servers

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos state
It reads alone and applies nothing, as ws client macos apply --check does, and needs what it needs: values.yml, and not root. The Mac's steps report the packages, services and files ws manages there, and a step that fails stands in the report as a check. The exit code stays 0. With several servers, one whose file ws refuses stands in the report with the refusal. A report holds no secret.

The text is on stdout and ws's own lines on stderr. --json prints the version of ws, then each step with its items and changes.

Usage:
  ws client macos state [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for state
      --json   print the report as JSON

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos tunnel
the Mac's tunnel to ws

Usage:
  ws client macos tunnel [flags]
  ws client macos tunnel [command]

Available Commands:
  key         make the Mac's key where none is, and print the public key, as root
  loaded      succeed where launchd has the tunnel's daemon loaded
  routes      route the networks into the tunnel, or drop NAME
  run         keep the tunnel to a server up, as root
  status      print the way in use and the peer, as root

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for tunnel

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one

Use "ws client macos tunnel [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client macos tunnel key
The private key stays in root's file, which only root can write, so setup runs this through sudo.

Usage:
  ws client macos tunnel key [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for key

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos tunnel loaded
Anyone may read launchd's system domain, so it needs no sudo.

Usage:
  ws client macos tunnel loaded [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for loaded

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos tunnel routes
It has the tunnel of --server, which is required, take the networks of NAME, or drop the file of NAME where it gets none.

Usage:
  ws client macos tunnel routes NAME [CIDR...] [flags]

Flags:
      --check   print each change and make none
  -h, --help    help for routes

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos tunnel run
The daemon logs to stdout, launchd's log file. SIGHUP reads the settings again, and SIGTERM stops it.

Usage:
  ws client macos tunnel run [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for run

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos tunnel status
print the way in use and the peer, as root

Usage:
  ws client macos tunnel status [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for status

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client macos values
print the Mac's values

Usage:
  ws client macos values [flags]
  ws client macos values [command]

Available Commands:
  print       print the checked settings of values.yml and a server's file as shell assignments

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for values

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one

Use "ws client macos values [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client macos values print
It reads FILE, else $WS_VALUES, else /etc/ws/values.yml, and the server's file, servers/NAME/values.yml beside it, of the server that --server names, and prints the checked settings as shell assignments, or the errors of a file it refuses.

Usage:
  ws client macos values print [FILE] [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help    help for print
      --lines   check each line alone

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws server
the server's systems

Usage:
  ws server [flags]
  ws server [command]

Available Commands:
  ubuntu      the commands for an Ubuntu server

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for server

Use "ws server [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws server ubuntu
the commands for an Ubuntu server

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu [flags]
  ws server ubuntu [command]

Available Commands:
  apply       set the server up, each role as Go steps
  cable       the cable role's check and reload, which its units run
  fans        drive the board's fans on the curves of CONFIG until SIGTERM
  secrets     keep ws's secrets file, with its identity as a systemd credential
  state       report what the server holds of what ws manages, and what apply would change
  tunnel      the tunnel role's check and wg0's key, which its units run
  values      edit the machine's values

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for ubuntu

Use "ws server ubuntu [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws server ubuntu apply
--secrets reads an age-encrypted TOML file with the secrets. The X25519 identity comes from the unit's credential ws-secrets (systemd's LoadCredential), in $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY.

--only names a role, or a step of it as ROLE/STEP, by the name that the run shows. A role's other steps are planned and left out, and a step that waits on one of them with changes is skipped.

The last line names the roles that changed.

--json prints each event of the run as a line of JSON on stdout: planned, started, done, failed or skipped, with the step, the first line of each change and the error. ws's own lines stay on stderr.

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu apply [flags]

Flags:
      --check          plan the steps and apply none
  -h, --help           help for apply
      --json           print each event of the run as a line of JSON on stdout
      --only strings   run the named roles and ROLE/STEPs alone, as a list or by repeating the flag
      --secrets FILE   read the secrets from the age-encrypted FILE
== ws server ubuntu cable
the cable role's check and reload, which its units run

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu cable [flags]
  ws server ubuntu cable [command]

Available Commands:
  check       refuse a cable's port that is an uplink, and a netplan FILE that would not set it up alone
  reload      put netplan's files in force where the cable's file changed since the last reload

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for cable

Use "ws server ubuntu cable [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws server ubuntu cable check
It reads the values of /etc/ws/values.yml. With no WS_CABLE_PORT there is no cable, and it passes. It fails where the port is missing, carries a default route or holds a lease from DHCP, or where networkd does not run on a booted machine.

With FILE, the cable's new netplan file, it also merges FILE with netplan's other files in a scratch root, and fails where netplan refuses them or would hand the port to another entry. It reads FILE as root, and no message quotes it. The cable role's guard runs it, and its file's check with the new file.

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu cable check [FILE] [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for check
== ws server ubuntu cable reload
/var/lib/ws/cable-in-force holds the SHA-256 of the cable's file that the last reload put in force. Where the file's differs, as where it is new, changed or gone, netplan generate writes networkd's files and networkctl reload sets up each link whose file changed; then the mark takes the file's SHA-256, or goes with the file. A failed reload leaves the mark, so the reload stays due. Another netplan file's change never makes it due. Without booted systemd nothing is due. Before a reload it checks the live cable file as cable check FILE does, so that another netplan file that takes the port never goes in force with it.

--check fails where a reload is due, and changes nothing. The cable role's run state runs it, after the cable's file.

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu cable reload [flags]

Flags:
      --check   fail where a reload is due, and change nothing
  -h, --help    help for reload
== ws server ubuntu fans
CONFIG is the file that the fans role writes, /etc/ws/fans.json by default.

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu fans [CONFIG] [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for fans
== ws server ubuntu secrets
keep ws's secrets file, with its identity as a systemd credential

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets [flags]
  ws server ubuntu secrets [command]

Available Commands:
  init        make ws's age identity and an empty secrets file
  keys        print each entry as KIND NAME, never a value
  set         set an entry, whose value never goes in an argument
  unset       drop an entry

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for secrets

Use "ws server ubuntu secrets [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws server ubuntu secrets init
It makes ws's age identity in memory, which systemd-creds encrypts by the host's key as the credential /etc/credstore.encrypted/ws-secrets, for a unit's LoadCredentialEncrypted=ws-secrets. It makes an empty /etc/ws/secrets.age, root's and 600. A credential or a file that is there stays.

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets init [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for init
== ws server ubuntu secrets keys
print each entry as KIND NAME, never a value

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets keys [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for keys
== ws server ubuntu secrets set
set an entry, whose value never goes in an argument

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets set [flags]
  ws server ubuntu secrets set [command]

Available Commands:
  env         set the env NAME, from a prompt with no echo or from stdin
  file        set a profile file (vpn_p12, vpn_sswan) from the file at PATH, or from stdin
  git         set a git server's token, from a prompt with no echo or from stdin
  password    set the password NAME, from a prompt with no echo or from stdin
  token       set the token NAME, from a prompt with no echo or from stdin

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for set

Use "ws server ubuntu secrets set [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws server ubuntu secrets set env
set the env NAME, from a prompt with no echo or from stdin

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets set env NAME [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for env
== ws server ubuntu secrets set file
set a profile file (vpn_p12, vpn_sswan) from the file at PATH, or from stdin

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets set file NAME [PATH] [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for file
== ws server ubuntu secrets set git
set a git server's token, from a prompt with no echo or from stdin

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets set git URL [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help              help for git
      --proactive-auth    send the token before the server asks for it, else as the entry does
      --username string   the user name that git sends with the token, else the entry's
== ws server ubuntu secrets set password
set the password NAME, from a prompt with no echo or from stdin

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets set password NAME [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for password
== ws server ubuntu secrets set token
set the token NAME, from a prompt with no echo or from stdin

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets set token NAME [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for token
== ws server ubuntu secrets unset
drop an entry

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu secrets unset KIND NAME [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for unset
== ws server ubuntu state
It reads alone and applies nothing. It needs what --check needs: root, on Ubuntu 26.04. It reports what ws manages and not the rest of the machine: the packages, services and files of the steps, what stands against them, and the changes that apply would make. A step that fails stands in the report as a check, and the exit code stays 0. It takes no secrets, so a report holds none and says nothing of what they write.

The text is on stdout and ws's own lines on stderr. --json prints the version of ws, then each role with its steps, items and changes.

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu state [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help           help for state
      --json           print the report as JSON
      --only strings   report the named roles and ROLE/STEPs alone, as a list or by repeating the flag
== ws server ubuntu tunnel
the tunnel role's check and wg0's key, which its units run

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu tunnel [flags]
  ws server ubuntu tunnel [command]

Available Commands:
  check       refuse a networkd that does not run, and a peer's key file that holds no key
  key         make wg0's private key where it is missing, and mend its owner, group and mode

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for tunnel

Use "ws server ubuntu tunnel [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws server ubuntu tunnel check
It reads the values of /etc/ws/values.yml. It fails where networkd does not run on a booted machine, as nothing would create wg0, or where a peer's /etc/wireguard/NAME.pub holds no WireGuard public key. It names each peer whose file is missing, which wg0 leaves out, and prints the server's public key where the key exists. The tunnel role's guard runs it, before wg0's key and files.

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu tunnel check [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for check
== ws server ubuntu tunnel key
The key is /etc/systemd/network/60-wg0.key, root:systemd-network 0640, which networkd reads. It is made once, and never shown: the run that makes it prints its public key. A file that holds no private key fails.

--check fails where the key is to be made or mended, and changes nothing. The tunnel role's run state runs it, after the guard.

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu tunnel key [flags]

Flags:
      --check   fail where the key is to be made or mended, and change nothing
  -h, --help    help for key
== ws server ubuntu values
edit the machine's values

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu values [flags]
  ws server ubuntu values [command]

Available Commands:
  set         write one key of /etc/ws/values.yml

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for values

Use "ws server ubuntu values [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws server ubuntu values set
An empty VALUE takes the key's line out, which gives its default back. A list's VALUE is split by commas. A table, such as WS_FANS_CURVES, stays the file's to edit by hand.

The next ws server ubuntu apply takes the key.

Usage:
  ws server ubuntu values set KEY VALUE [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for set
== ws unit
plan and apply the units of ws's kinds, KIND/NAME, for mise

Usage:
  ws unit [flags]
  ws unit [command]

Available Commands:
  list        print each unit, KIND/NAME, in the order the units run
  read        print each unit's state: in-sync, or the first change that write would make
  verify      fail where mise's plan lacks a unit of the specs
  write       apply the units, in the order they run

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for unit

Use "ws unit [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws unit list
The specs are each units.toml one level below WS_UNITS, or below /opt/ws/roles/server/ubuntu where it is unset. --toml prints each unit as a line of mise's [bootstrap.packages].

Usage:
  ws unit list [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for list
      --toml   print the units as lines of mise's [bootstrap.packages]
== ws unit read
It plans each UNIT and the units it waits on, and applies nothing. It prints a line per UNIT on stdout, in the order given: the unit, a tab and its state. The state is in-sync; the headline of its first change, with " (+N more)" for the rest; "after UNIT" where it waits on a unit with changes; or "fails: " and why its plan failed. A failed plan stops no other unit's read. ws's own lines go to stderr.

Usage:
  ws unit read UNIT... [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for read
== ws unit verify
It reads mise's packages, by mise bootstrap packages status --json, and fails where the ws plugin is not loaded or a unit of the specs is not among its packages: mise drops a refused plugin's units with no error. It changes nothing. Where each unit is there, it prints a line that does not change.

Usage:
  ws unit verify [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for verify
== ws unit write
It plans, applies and plans again each UNIT, in the order the units run whatever the order given. A unit that a UNIT waits on and that is not named is planned and left out. A UNIT behind one with changes is skipped, and fails the write. --dry-run applies nothing and prints each change, file diffs included. ws's lines go to stderr, and the last one names the units that changed.

Usage:
  ws unit write UNIT... [flags]

Flags:
      --dry-run   plan the units and apply none
  -h, --help      help for write
== ws update
Downloads the latest release's ws for this system, checks it against the release's ws.sha256 and that it runs, then replaces the file ws runs from, the file a symbolic link leads to.

Where ws is the latest release already, or newer, nothing changes. A ws built from a clone is refused.

Usage:
  ws update [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for update
== ws version
print the version of this ws

Usage:
  ws version [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for version
